[s0l0!-header][h1]The #define Directive[/h1][/s0l0!-header]

The [b]#define[/b] directive is used to create object-like macros for constants based on values or expressions. 
#define can also be used to create function-like macros with arguments that will be replaced by the preprocessor.

Be cautious with function-like definitions. Keep in mind that the preprocessor does a direct replacement without any calculations, which can lead to unexpected results, as demonstrated with the following program:[code format="c" codeId="2209"][b]#include <stdio.h>
#define PI 3.14
#define AREA(r) (PI*r*r)
[/b]
int main() {
  float radius = 2;
  printf("%3.2f\n", [b]PI[/b]);
  printf("Area is %5.2f\n", [b]AREA(radius)[/b]);
  printf("Area with radius + 1: %5.2f\n", [b]AREA(radius+1)[/b]);
  return 0;
} [/code]
Before compilation, the preprocessor expands every macro identifier. In this case, every occurrence of [b]PI[/b] is replaced with 3.14 and [b]AREA[/b](arg) is replaced with the expression PI*arg*arg. The final code sent to the compiler will already have the constant values in place.

Not what we may expect! However, if you consider that #define works strictly by replacing text, you will see that AREA(radius+1) becomes PI*radius+1*radius+1, which is 3.14*2+1*2+1.

The solution to this is to enclose each parameter in parentheses to obtain the correct order of operations.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="2210"]#define AREA(r) (PI*(r)*(r)) [/code]
[note]The code produces the output: Area with radius + 1: 28.26.[/note]

