[s0l0!-header][h1]Predefined Macro Definitions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

In addition to defining your own macros, there are several standard [b]predefined macros[/b] that are always available in a C program without requiring the #define directive:
[b]__DATE__[/b]  The current date as a string in the format Mm dd yyyy
[b]__TIME__[/b]  The current time as a string in the format hh:mm:ss
[b]__FILE__[/b]  The current filename as a string
[b]__LINE__[/b] The current line number as an int value
[b]__STDC__[/b] 1

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="2215"]char curr_time[10];
char curr_date[12];
int std_c;

strcpy(curr_time, __TIME__);
strcpy(curr_date, __DATE__);
printf("%s %s\n", curr_time, curr_date);
printf("This is line %d\n", __LINE__);    
std_c = __STDC__;
printf("STDC is %d", std_c); [/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

